      sections: [{"id":"s1","title":"Keyboard Layouts","whyItMatters":"Your keyboard layout affects speed, comfort, and long-term hand health.","content":"**QWERTY (1873):**\n- Designed to prevent jams on typewriters\n- Most common, lowest learning curve\n- 57% of typing on left hand\n- Some finger travel inefficiency\n\n**Dvorak (1936):**\n- Optimized for efficiency\n- 70% of typing on home row\n- Alternating hands for rhythm\n- Steep learning curve, 40% less finger travel\n\n**Colemak (2006):**\n- Modern alternative to QWERTY\n- Only 17 key changes from QWERTY\n- Retains common shortcuts (Ctrl+C/V/Z)\n- Good balance of efficiency and learnability\n\n**Ergonomic Keyboards:**\n- Split keyboards (ergodox, moonlander)\n- Ortholinear (plank, preonic)\n- Column-staggered instead of row-staggered\n\n**Recommendation:** Stick with QWERTY unless you have pain or want challenge.","codeExamples":[{"id":"ex1","title":"Layout Comparison","description":"Type the same phrase on different layout patterns","code":{"typing-speed":"qwerty: the quick brown fox\ndvorak: ei uicka brawn ioc\ncolemak: the qulck brown kox"},"explanation":"Notice the finger travel differences between layouts."}]}],
