      sections: [{"id":"s1","title":"IDE Efficiency","whyItMatters":"A developer's typing speed isn't just WPM — it's how efficiently they use the IDE to produce code.","content":"**Essential Keyboard Shortcuts (VS Code):**\n- Ctrl+P: Quick file open\n- Ctrl+Shift+P: Command palette\n- Ctrl+D: Select next occurrence\n- Ctrl+/: Toggle comment\n- Alt+Up/Down: Move line\n- Ctrl+Shift+K: Delete line\n- Ctrl+Space: Trigger suggestion\n- F12: Go to definition\n\n**Multi-Cursor Typing:**\n- Alt+Click: Add cursor\n- Ctrl+Alt+Up/Down: Add cursor above/below\n- Ctrl+Shift+L: Select all occurrences\n\n**Snippet Patterns:**\nSnippets let you type 4-5 chars and get 20+ lines:\n- `clg` → `console.log()`\n- `fori` → `for (let i = 0; i < ; i++) {}`\n- `arr =>` → arrow function\n\n**Key Insight:** A developer who uses shortcuts and snippets effectively can produce code 2-3x faster than raw WPM suggests.","codeExamples":[{"id":"ex1","title":"Shortcut Practice","description":"Type code that exercises shortcut-friendly patterns","code":{"typing-speed":"console.log(); console.log(); console.log();\nconst add = (a, b) => a + b;\nconst sub = (a, b) => a - b;\nconst mul = (a, b) => a * b;\nif (x > 0 && x < 100) { return true; }\nif (y > 0 && y < 100) { return false; }"},"explanation":"These patterns are prime candidates for multi-cursor editing."}]}],
      quiz: { questions: [{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","question":"What shortcut selects all occurrences of the current selection?","options":["Ctrl+D","Ctrl+Shift+L","Ctrl+F","Alt+Click"],"correctAnswer":"Ctrl+Shift+L","explanation":"Ctrl+Shift+L selects all occurrences at once for multi-cursor editing.","difficulty":2}], passingScore: 70 },
      cheatSheet: [{"label":"Ctrl+P","value":"Quick file open"},{"label":"Ctrl+D","value":"Select next occurrence"},{"label":"Ctrl+/","value":"Toggle comment"}]
